# Rule 6 of the product direction (issue #3; QUESTIONS Q17): drags must never block input.
# Story: somebody drags windows about, slowly, quickly, with ⌘ held, and once Arbor is paused with a window in the hand.
# However a drag ends, nothing of Arbor's is left on the screen that could take the next click.
# This is the half that needs no Mac: the preview is up only while a window is in the hand. That the panel lets the mouse through
# is board 1.6.
# As the first build shipped it: a docked window is dragged out onto the Desktop beside another.
set desktopColumn true
quiet
# The three tiling switches of the guide's section 2 are off: with macOS's own tiling on, the left, right and top edges of the screen are macOS's (Q23).
mac-tiling off
open Safari
open Notes
open Mail
drag 101 2 400
expect docked 101
expect preview none
drag 102 1438 400
expect docked 102
expect preview none
# Let go at once, before any target has woken up.
drag 103 1000 850 quick
expect preview none
# With ⌘ nothing docks and nothing is offered.
drag 103 2 300 cmd
expect floating 103
expect preview none
# Out of a slot and back onto the Desktop.
drag 102 720 400
expect floating 102
expect preview none
# Dropped on another window's edge.
drag 102 300 850
expect docked 102
expect preview none
# The control: while a window is held over a target there is a preview to lose.
pick-up 103 1438 400
expect preview showing
let-go
expect docked 103
expect preview none
# Arbor is paused with a window in the hand.
pick-up 103 2 400
expect preview showing
pause
# The drag is called off, and the window goes straight to its own frame, not by way of its place.
expect preview none
let-go
resume
expect preview none
